{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":2,"output":7,"cacheRead":18152,"cacheWrite":1699,"totalTokens":19860,"cost":{"input":2.0000000000000002e-7,"output":0.0000035,"cacheRead":0.00018152,"cacheWrite":0.000212375,"total":0.00039759500000000004,"totalFromProvider":false},"cacheWrite1h":0},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"I'm checking whether the collapse shows up at widths 40, 45, and 60. It worked at 60 but collapsed at 45. The fixed columns of 22 and 18 plus padding come to 46, which is already over 45, so the flexible space goes negative and the flex minimum of 3 takes over. The table then ends up wider than the maximum, and the collapse step shrinks the wrappable columns, Load included.\n\nI'm working out why the Load column ends up at width 0: the collapse step shrinks the widest columns first, but nothing stops it from pushing a flexible column like Load below its minimum. The fix I'm considering is giving _collapse_widths a per-column minimum so flexible columns never drop under one character plus padding.\n\nI'm adjusting the collapse logic so flexible columns get a floor of padding plus one character. The planned change is to pass minimum widths into the reduction step, so a column like Load can't be shrunk below that floor even when the table overflows.\n\nI'm reworking the while loop in _collapse_widths so it stops reducing a column once it reaches its minimum width, since otherwise a column already at its floor would produce no change and the loop would never end. I'm considering limiting the wrappable set to columns wider than their minimum, and I'm thinking of making the collapse function take a minimum width for each column.\n\nI'm now rewriting `_collapse_widths` to take a per-column minimum, and only shrinking columns that are still above their floor, so the loop can't stall on a column already at its minimum.\n\nI'm now looking at how the collapse loop can shrink the max column: its reduction has to be capped at its distance above the minimum width, so it stops at its floor instead of going below it.\n\nI'm now working through the per-column floor in the collapse loop, letting each column shrink only as far as its own minimum width and its distance from the second-largest column. If the two largest columns are tied, the reduction stops and falls back to the even distribution.\n\nI'm weighing a cleaner design: when the flexible minimums can't fit, flexible columns can't collapse below their floor, so the table just overflows. Service and Host have explicit widths, so they aren't wrappable, which leaves only Load to collapse.\n\nI'm now checking whether a flexible column like Load can be kept at its minimum width when the table overflows, since marking it non-wrapable would still leave the last-resort even reduction cutting it below that floor.\n\nI'm noting that with the edge borders the fixed columns already total 48, more than the 45 available, so something has to give. Explicit widths are user-set and shouldn't shrink, so the last-resort reduction should skip flexible columns already at their minimum.\n\nI'm now reasoning about the last-resort reduction: capping each column's shrink at its width minus its minimum, so flexible columns like Load stop at their floor. If the excess still can't be absorbed, the table will overflow and get cropped at render time, and the fixed-width columns will take the shrink, as they already do.\n\nI'm checking how the minimum width interacts with the measurement: if the column width includes padding, then a width of 3 leaves one character of content, so the minimum of one character plus padding holds. The overflow case with flexible minimums should also just work, since the distribution clamps each column to its minimum.\n\nI'm checking whether empty tables could hit a problem when the pad distribution runs on column widths that are all zero, since the ratios would then all be zero and the assertion would trip.\n\nI'm seeing that empty tables with expand enabled would crash, since distributing padding across zero columns hits an assertion. The ZeroDivisionError in ratio_resolve shouldn't happen, because zero ratios are treated as one, so the flexible ratio sum can't be zero.\n\nI'm now looking at whether ratio_distribute can divide by zero when every ratio is zero. Its guard only checks for a positive total, so I'm considering treating all-zero ratios as equal so padding still gets spread across columns instead of tripping the assertion.\n\nI'm adjusting `ratio_distribute` so it no longer asserts on empty lists or all-zero ratios, and I'm considering a fallback that spreads the distribution evenly in that case.\n\nI'm going to make `ratio_distribute` handle empty lists and all-zero ratios by falling back to an even split, so the padding step stops tripping its assertion on empty or collapsed tables. I'm also noting that a negative remaining width would hand flexible columns values below their minimums, so I'll need to clamp that case too.\n\nI'm checking how the minimum clamp interacts with the distribution step, and whether a negative remaining width could push flexible columns under their floors. Clamping the total at zero looks like the safe move, and I'm also wondering whether any existing ratio_distribute tests depend on negative totals.\n\nI'm now defining minimum widths for the collapse step, so flexible columns keep their floor rather than being shrunk below it. Only wrappable columns are reduced, and any column without a minimum gets zero.\n\nI'm working out the expected result at width 45: with explicit widths on Service and Host, only Load can wrap, but it's already at its minimum, so the last-resort reduction has to take the excess from Service and Host instead.\n\nI'm checking the last-resort reduction next: `ratio_reduce` only shrinks a column while its maximum allows, so zeroing the ratio for columns at their minimum should keep Load from dropping below its floor.\n\nNow I'm checking the expand-padding step, where pad gets distributed across the column widths. If every column is zero wide, as in an empty table, the ratios would all be zero, which is the case the fallback has to handle.\n\nI'm making `_collapse_widths` accept minimum widths so flexible columns stop at their floor, then checking how `ratio_distribute` behaves with empty lists and all-zero ratios. I'll check the tests for `_collapse_widths` before changing its signature.\n\nI'm making the minimum widths a full per-column list, with flexible columns floored at their content width plus padding and everything else at zero. Explicit widths on flexible columns only serve as that floor.\n\nI'm checking whether `_resolve` can divide by zero, and whether a zero ratio gets treated inconsistently between the denominator and the portion. I'll guard the denominator explicitly and check how a negative remaining width is handled.\n\nI'm going to check the tests that reference these functions before changing anything.\n\n"}}
